A||||7||arktoon||GRAND-AM 2,5 engine into a FIERO ?
Z||000000||arktoon||07-14-99||04:39 AM||||I have problems with my 2,5 engine (almost everything is broken, cracked, etc.), and I could find nothing original to put instead, but I found a 2,5L 4cyl. engine from a '91 GRAND-AM. It is in very good shape, only 25.000 miles (42.000 km)in it. Please help me to know does it fit to my FIERO or not.<br>My mechanical says, it does not, but the owner of the GRAND-AM, who is also a mechanical (more famous), says it has to be good. Every trailing of the car is lot of money, so I would like to know the true before the next step. In HUNGARY we don't know that much the possibilities.<p>Thanks for helping, and thanks for standing my poor english.
Z||000001||ChadMan||07-14-99||07:18 AM||||I'm curious about this too, Arktoon.  According to the Chilton's manual, the '91 2.5 makes 110 hp - 12 more than the 87-88 Fiero and 18 more than the 84-86.  That's not a big gain, but it's significant.  The redline is also supposed to be higher (5500 vs. 5000).  Where does the extra power come from?  New head design?  Internal mods (con rods, bearings)?<p>My guess is that the 91 engine will bolt right up to the Fiero's motor mounts and transaxle, but you'll need the ECM and wiring harnesses from the donor car.  And if the head is different, it may not bolt up to the Fiero's exhaust manifold.  But that's just a guess...
Z||000002||theogre||07-14-99||08:10 AM||||as long as it uses the same base block design, it should work. You may need the ECM and wires, take them from the donor just for safety.... you will need to change the pully on the waterpump, the idler unit and most of the brackets.<p>If your car is not 87-88 then you will definatly need the ECM and wire to get the thing in. In 87 the ECM, TBI, and many connectors changed. The pre 87 ECM is not compatible with the 87+TBI.<p>I think the ECM is still the same in 91, but it may have a diferant chip to boost the HP. If the thing is now MPFI instead of TBI then you'll also have to do the ECM/wire swap.<p>So basically, it should fit physically, but electrically you could have allot of additional work.
Z||000003||batboy||07-14-99||06:12 PM||||You probably can use the Grand Am's basic engine and use all of the Fiero's pulleys, accessories, fuel injection, electronics, etc. This might be easier than using all the donor car stuff, maybe not. Sometime around that time period, 2.5 engines got roller cam and lifters, this may account for the higher horsepower rating.
Z||000004||arktoon||07-15-99||12:11 AM||||Thanks for everybody. I'll try it.
Z||000005||theogre||07-15-99||06:31 PM||||Roller cam started in 86. 700 TBI, and new ECM, in 87.<p>To use the earlier TBI requires changing the intake manifold.<p>Using any FWD motor in Fiero requires changing all the brackets, etc.
Z||000006||Hock||07-16-99||12:15 PM||||IF you really want to know call a salvage yard, they can tell you. Now if you really don't care what the book says then do it. The 2.5 out of the Grand Am is an U code engine. It is a totally different engine than the one out of the Fiero. <p>Also, Orge, so you know the Roller Cam was introduced in 1985. It became standard in all 2.5L engines.
Z||000007||arktoon||07-16-99||12:40 PM||||What is code "U" ? And what code no. FIERO has ?
